@@2
From: Europa Sysop
To: All
Subj: The leap-day crash: what happened
Date: 06-19-88 22:15

Several of you asked, so here is the whole story, once, and then I'd
like never to think about it again.

At 2:10 AM on February 29th the northbound freight came through, as it
does every night.  The house shook, as it does every night.  The old
10 MB drive, which had been making a noise like a coffee grinder for a
week, chose that moment to put its heads down on the platter and keep
them there.

I heard it from the kitchen.  There is no sound quite like it.

The good news: I had a floppy backup of the user file, 38 disks, made
the Sunday before.  The bad news: nothing else.  It took a weekend and
four pizzas to put the user file back together (disk 23 had a bad
sector; Karen Whitfield sat on the floor with me and retyped eleven
records from a printout).

Lessons, for free:

  1. Back up.  Then back up the backup.
  2. A disk that sounds different IS different.
  3. Do not put your computer on the same wall as the railroad.

The new machine sits on a foam pad on the OTHER side of the room.  It
was down "a few weeks".  It was three months.  I know.

On the bright side, it happened on February 29th, a day that only
exists one year in four.  So technically, it never happened.

-- Europa
